:: Walked and ran for half an hour, arriving back {"position":"1500,250","size":"100,100"}
After about half an hour of trekking through the relentless heat—a mix of determined walking and desperate, drink-saving sprints—my willpower had melted faster than the ice. I couldn't help sipping more of my own drink. The craving won again. And again.
Then, my eyes drifted to the second cup in the bag, my roommate's sacred trust. Just a taste, I reasoned, to make sure it's still good. One sip won't hurt, I thought. It hurt so good. I took another. The heat was my accomplice, my excuse. Before I knew it, I was staring at a cup that was conspicuously less than full.

"Damn," I whispered to the empty street, a bubble of guilty laughter escaping my lips. "I almost finished the second cup." I giggled, a truly evil sound even to my own ears, the petty crime feeling absurd and thrilling. The small, nagging guilt was utterly drowned out by the sweet, tangy victory on my tongue.
With a deep, theatrical breath to compose myself, I finally slid the key into our door and pushed it open, the evidence held casually with an innocnet smile on my face. I stepped into the room with cooling air conditioning on.


[[Deliver the drink to my roomate.]]
